Plastic flow, hardening and load history (@mech-plasticity: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @mechanical-engineering, #source "../sources/Mechanics-of-Materials-Roylance.lingua#r_7K2RYA9504FGFGJTVWHMWXD5WY", #source "../sources/Applied-Mechanics-of-Solids-Bower.lingua#r_109WFZBWMEMGHV9TGFD22H2F1W", #mechanical-engineering "Viscoelasticity-creep-relaxation-and-temperature.lingua#r_5EQ8VH9QC9479TD3ZKYFFN8P56") { r_68855TQ5FAEA06A1AVTP8ZNZAT
**Read:** [Modules in Mechanics of Materials — David Roylance](../sources/Mechanics-of-Materials-Roylance.lingua#r_7K2RYA9504FGFGJTVWHMWXD5WY), Yield and Plastic Flow and Dislocation Basis of Yield and Creep; [Applied Mechanics of Solids — Allan F. Bower](../sources/Applied-Mechanics-of-Solids-Bower.lingua#r_109WFZBWMEMGHV9TGFD22H2F1W), section 3.7 and chapter 6.

Plastic deformation remains after unloading in the model considered here. A yield surface specifies the stress states at which further plastic flow can begin, a flow rule specifies the direction of plastic strain increments, and a hardening law specifies how the state evolves. A uniaxial curve alone does not settle every multiaxial loading path.

For a simple uniaxial elastic–perfectly-plastic material, loading beyond σ_y leaves elastic strain σ_y/E plus plastic strain. On unloading elastically to zero stress, the elastic part recovers. If E = 200 GPa, σ_y = 250 MPa and total tensile strain reaches 0.005, the model predicts residual strain 0.00375. This ignores hardening, damage and rate effects.

Isotropic hardening expands an ideal yield surface; kinematic hardening translates it and can represent aspects of reversed-loading behavior. Local yielding can redistribute loads in a redundant structure, but sufficient rotation capacity and stability must be established before using a plastic-collapse model.

**Work:** Construct and integrate a loading–unloading–reversal history with two different hardening laws. Track internal variables, plastic work and energy consistency. Compare predicted residual displacement and reverse yield with a documented experiment, and explain what additional data would distinguish the laws.

